; ======================================================================
;  thermal.pi4 - how hot the BCM2711 is, from a LOADED DRIVER and from
;  the firmware, with the driver preferred and the firmware as the
;  fallback.
;
;  THE REGISTER HALF OF THIS FILE BECAME A MODULE ON 2026-09-10.
;
;  It used to read the SoC's AVS ring-oscillator temperature monitor
;  itself: an address, two validity bits and a straight-line conversion,
;  all written down here. Those facts did not change and they did not go
;  away - they moved, whole and with their citations, into
;  RaspberryPi4/Modules/thermal_avs.pi4, which is compiled separately
;  into THERMAL.MOD and loaded off the card at boot.
;
;  WHY, AND IT IS RULING 2 OF THE DRIVER-MODULE DESIGN: the core keeps
;  only what reaches the card. Everyone is not going to have the same
;  hardware, and a monitor that carries every part's temperature sensor
;  compiled in is a monitor that grows one driver at a time forever. A
;  temperature sensor is about as far from the boot path as a driver on
;  this board gets - nothing that loads a module needs it - which is
;  exactly why it went first.
;
;  TWO SOURCES, AND THEY ARE STILL NOT INTERCHANGEABLE
;
;    THE SEAM. A loaded driver publishes one function that answers the
;    part's temperature in millidegrees Celsius. On this board that is a
;    plain register read: no mailbox, no buffer, no cache maintenance,
;    nothing that can be starved by a busy firmware. It answers in about
;    the time a load takes, which is what makes a fan policy that runs on
;    every tick affordable. WITH NO MODULE LOADED THE SEAM IS EMPTY and
;    this file says so rather than pretending.
;
;    THE MAILBOX. GET_TEMPERATURE, which this tree has been asking the
;    firmware since the `info` command existed. It costs a property-
;    buffer round trip, it can be starved (see mailbox.pi4's notes on
;    the framebuffer and the MBOX1 wedge), and it is the ONLY source for
;    the throttle temperature - a temperature sensor reports what it
;    measures and has no "and this is where I start slowing down" field.
;    IT STAYS IN THE CORE. It is not a driver for a device; it is a
;    question asked of the firmware that is already here for other
;    reasons, and it is the fallback that makes the module optional.
;
;  SO: temperature comes from the seam when a driver fills it and answers,
;  and from the mailbox when it does not. The maximum comes from the
;  mailbox always. Which one answered is recorded and reported, because a
;  board that has quietly fallen back to the mailbox is a board whose fan
;  policy now costs a round trip per tick, and that should be visible
;  rather than deduced. THAT REPORT IS NOW ALSO HOW AN OPERATOR SEES THAT
;  A MODULE IS DOING SOMETHING: `fan` naming the direct source is the
;  visible difference between THERMAL.MOD loaded and not.
;
;  THE FALLBACK IS WHY THIS CONVERSION IS SAFE TO DO WITHOUT A BOARD. A
;  board with no THERMAL.MOD on its card behaves exactly as this tree did
;  before the module existed, because the mailbox path is untouched and
;  is what answered whenever the register was invalid anyway. The module
;  can only restore the faster source, never remove the slower one.
;
;  EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE IS CELSIUS, AND NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS
;  EVER PRINTED. Project ruling, 2026-09-07: every temperature a person
;  reads is Fahrenheit. This library is the half of that rule that keeps
;  its Celsius, and deliberately: the firmware answers GET_TEMPERATURE in
;  millidegrees Celsius and the seam carries millidegrees Celsius, so a
;  file rewritten into Fahrenheit could no longer be read line by line
;  against the documents quoted in the driver. Convert at the edge, not
;  in the maths.
;
;  THE CONVERSION IS Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi's HwTempMilliF()/HwTempWholeF(),
;  once, for the whole tree.
;
;  REQUIRES, in this order, in the main file (house rule 10):
;      Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi                            ; #SVCCAP_THERMAL
;      Anvil/Hal/module_format.pbi
;      Anvil/Hal/module_runtime.pbi                   ; #MOD_TEMP_NONE
;      Anvil/Core/mod_registry.pbi                    ; the seam table
;      RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4                   ; MailboxTemperature
;      RaspberryPi4/Lib/thermal.pi4
;
;  HARDWARE-VERIFIED (2026-09-06, pi4-fan-silicon), and the measurement
;  belongs to the driver that now carries it: `fan` on the board printed
;  39 C (39920 millidegrees) sourced from "a sensor register the board
;  reads directly", alongside the firmware's 85 C throttle point from the
;  mailbox. The AVS address, the two validity bits and the negative slope
;  are therefore measured and not only cited - in
;  RaspberryPi4/Modules/thermal_avs.pi4, where they are now.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SOURCES, and #THERM_NONE.
;
;  #THERM_NONE is not zero and not -1, and both of those matter. Zero is
;  a real temperature (this part in a freezer) and -1 is a real
;  temperature (colder still); a sentinel that collides with a reading
;  is how a fan ends up off in a hot room. -273151 is a millidegree
;  value below absolute zero, so it can never be a measurement, and it
;  is far enough from every real reading that a caller comparing with
;  the wrong operator gets an obviously absurd number rather than a
;  nearly-right one.
;
;  IT IS THE SAME NUMBER AS #MOD_TEMP_NONE and they are still two
;  definitions in two files, translated at the boundary below rather
;  than shared - the same discipline #HW_TEMP_NONE is already under,
;  and for the same reason: a future divergence becomes a compile-time
;  question instead of a silent one.
;
;  #THERM_SRC_REGISTER KEPT ITS NAME AND ITS NUMBER through the module
;  conversion, because it names what the OPERATOR sees - a reading that
;  came straight off the part rather than through the firmware - and
;  that did not change when the code that does it moved into a file of
;  its own. Renaming it would have renamed a line in `fan`'s output for
;  an internal reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#THERM_NONE = -273151

#THERM_SRC_NONE    = 0        ; nothing answered
#THERM_SRC_REGISTER = 1       ; a driver reading the part directly
#THERM_SRC_MAILBOX  = 2       ; the firmware property interface

Global therm_source.i = #THERM_SRC_NONE
Global therm_seamFails.i = 0  ; how many times the seam gave no reading
Global therm_bind.i = -1      ; the binding held on the thermal seam

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalSaySelf() - who is holding the seam, for `mod unload`'s refusal.
;
;  A printing procedure rather than a string because the core owns the
;  layout of the refusal line and the consumer owns the words in it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ThermalSaySelf()
  Print("the monitor's temperature readings")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalSeamDetach() - give the seam back.
;
;  This is the other half of a binding and it is the half that makes a
;  binding honest. A consumer that takes a service and offers no way to
;  release it has not made `mod unload` refusable, it has made it
;  impossible - and an unload that can never succeed is the same wrong
;  answer as one that always does, wearing a safety label.
;
;  AFTER IT RUNS, TEMPERATURE STILL WORKS. The next read finds the seam
;  empty and falls back to the firmware, which is exactly the state a
;  board with no module on its card is in. Nothing anywhere has to be
;  told; that is what the fallback is for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ThermalSeamDetach()
  If therm_bind >= 0
    ModSeamUnbind(therm_bind)
    therm_bind = -1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalSeamMilliC() - the loaded driver's answer, or #THERM_NONE.
;
;  THE CALL IS BRACKETED BY ModSeamEnter/ModSeamLeave. That pair is not a
;  lock and is not reentrancy protection - this monitor runs one flow of
;  control and the compiler gives a procedure static storage for its
;  locals, so two calls into one driver at once would be wrong for
;  reasons no counter could fix. What the depth catches is the case that
;  CAN happen on one flow: a console command issued from inside a driver
;  callback asking to unload the driver it is running in. `mod unload`
;  refuses while the depth is non-zero, which is a return address into
;  memory about to be abandoned.
;
;  THE BINDING IS TAKEN ON THE FIRST SUCCESSFUL READ and not when the
;  module loads. Binding at load time would claim a service this file had
;  not yet decided to use; binding on first use says exactly what is
;  true, which is that the monitor's temperature readings now come from
;  here and something has to release them before the driver goes away.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalSeamMilliC()
  Define *fn
  Define t.i
  *fn = ModSeamFn(#SVCCAP_THERMAL, #SEAM_THERMAL_READ)
  If *fn = 0
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  ModSeamEnter(#SVCCAP_THERMAL)
  t = fn()
  ModSeamLeave(#SVCCAP_THERMAL)
  ; #MOD_TEMP_NONE and #THERM_NONE are the same number and are still
  ; translated. Two definitions in two files, one boundary.
  If t = #MOD_TEMP_NONE
    therm_seamFails = therm_seamFails + 1
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  If therm_bind < 0
    therm_bind = ModSeamBind(#SVCCAP_THERMAL, @ThermalSaySelf, @ThermalSeamDetach)
    If therm_bind < 0
      ; The registry is full of bindings. The READING IS STILL GOOD and
      ; is returned; what is lost is the ability to refuse an unload
      ; while this file is using it, which is a weaker guarantee and not
      ; a reason to report a board as too hot to read.
      therm_bind = -1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalMailboxMilliC() - the firmware path.
;
;  MailboxTemperature() returns the firmware's millidegrees and 0 when
;  the call failed - which is mailbox.pi4's convention and is fine there
;  because 0 C from this tag has never been seen and the caller can ask
;  MailboxError() why. It is NOT fine here, because this file's
;  no-answer value has to be one that cannot collide with a reading, so
;  a 0 from the mailbox is translated into #THERM_NONE.
;
;  THAT TRANSLATION LOSES A REAL 0 C READING and that is the right trade
;  in this direction: a Pi 4 at exactly 0.000 C would be reported as "no
;  answer" and the fan would fall back to the seam or to its own safe
;  default, which is correct behaviour for a freezing board. The
;  opposite trade - treating a failed call as 0 C - is a fan that never
;  starts.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalMailboxMilliC()
  Define t.i
  t = MailboxTemperature()
  If t = 0
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; ThermalMaxMilliC() - the temperature at which the firmware starts
; throttling. MAILBOX ONLY: a sensor reports what it measures and has no
; field for what the firmware intends to do about it, so there is no
; driver path to prefer here and none is pretended. It is also why this
; half of the file could not have gone into the module: a driver for a
; temperature block has nothing to say about it.
Procedure.i ThermalMaxMilliC()
  Define t.i
  t = MailboxTemperatureMax()
  If t = 0
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalMilliC() - THE ONE A CALLER WANTS. The loaded driver first, the
;  mailbox second, #THERM_NONE if neither answered, and ThermalSource()
;  says which it was.
;
;  THE SOURCE IS SET ON EVERY CALL, including the failing one. A stale
;  source left over from the last successful read would report the direct
;  path while the mailbox is quietly carrying the load - and now also
;  while no module is loaded at all, which is the one thing an operator
;  looking at `fan` needs this line to tell them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalMilliC()
  Define t.i
  t = ThermalSeamMilliC()
  If t <> #THERM_NONE
    therm_source = #THERM_SRC_REGISTER
    ProcedureReturn t
  EndIf
  t = ThermalMailboxMilliC()
  If t <> #THERM_NONE
    therm_source = #THERM_SRC_MAILBOX
    ProcedureReturn t
  EndIf
  therm_source = #THERM_SRC_NONE
  ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ThermalSource()
  ProcedureReturn therm_source
EndProcedure

; How many times the loaded driver was asked and had no valid reading.
; A rising count with a module loaded means the silicon is answering
; invalid, not that the module is missing - `mod` tells those apart.
Procedure.i ThermalRegisterFailures()
  ProcedureReturn therm_seamFails
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalWholeC(milli) - millidegrees to whole degrees, rounded to
;  NEAREST and correct on both sides of zero.
;
;  Integer division truncates toward zero in this language, so
;  (m + 500) / 1000 rounds correctly for positive m and wrongly for
;  negative m: -1600 would give -1 where the nearest whole degree is -2.
;  A Pi 4 below zero is unusual and not impossible - a board in an
;  unheated workshop in winter is the case that eventually turns up -
;  and a rounding rule that is only right in the common half is exactly
;  the kind of thing that is never noticed and never right.
;
;  #THERM_NONE passes through unchanged so a caller can print the same
;  sentinel test either side of the conversion.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalWholeC(milli.i)
  If milli = #THERM_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  If milli >= 0
    ProcedureReturn (milli + 500) / 1000
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -((-milli + 500) / 1000)
EndProcedure
